# Sliders and precise values

Use a slider to adjust a known, bounded value such as brightness or volume. Pair it with a precise text field and visible decrease and increase buttons when the task requires an exact value. All inputs represent one draft. Keep the last reported device value separate.

**This is an original civica implementation informed by an official UK range-slider reference.** The [Royal Navy Design System publishes a Range Slider](https://storybook.design-system.navy.digital.mod.uk/?path=/docs/components-range-slider--docs). It was found when the initial eight-catalogue review was widened. The [UK systems review](../community/uk-design-system-review.html) now records nine indexed systems and distinguishes current component evidence from legacy guidance and partial departmental checks.

GOV.UK [Question pages](https://design-system.service.gov.uk/patterns/question-pages/#using-range-sliders) advises against slider questions and requires a non-drag alternative if one is used. The local exception is adjustment of an existing device setting. It does not justify using a slider to collect a person's precise answer in an ordinary form.

## Royal Navy reference and local decisions

Royal Navy's current [component source at release 4.64.2](https://github.com/Royal-Navy/design-system/blob/13163badc75f09695bb48968a0f82928eead6d35/packages/react-component-library/src/components/RangeSlider/Slider.tsx) supports one or two handles, minimum/maximum bounds, steps, ticks, displayed units, custom formatting, disabled and reversed scales, and optional threshold tracks. Its [ten published stories](https://storybook.design-system.navy.digital.mod.uk/?path=/docs/components-range-slider--docs) include brightness icons and labelled scales. These are relevant numeric-input primitives. They do not specify a light's supported range, a heating limit, a request policy or a confirmed device result.

| Upstream primitive | civica decision |
| --- | --- |
| Bounded track, handle and displayed value | Keep the recognisable anatomy using native HTML range semantics and civica colours and focus |
| Steps and optional value formatting | Use the actual integration step and unit; preserve decimal precision in both the input and accessible value |
| Optional ticks and endpoint icons | Add only when useful; retain written labels, bounds and units so icons are not the only explanation |
| Two handles and crossing/pushing modes | Not implemented by these examples; use two labelled precise fields with ordering validation unless a dual-handle task has separate evidence |
| Coloured thresholds and reversed scales | Not adopted by default; direction and thresholds need an actual device meaning and independent validation |
| Disabled component | Keep the unavailable reason and previous reading separate; never present a numeric thumb position as an unknown observation |

The current [Handle source](https://github.com/Royal-Navy/design-system/blob/13163badc75f09695bb48968a0f82928eead6d35/packages/react-component-library/src/components/RangeSlider/Handle.tsx) uses the generic accessible name “Select range”, and the default formatter floors numeric values. The local design requires a specific value label and exact decimal formatting. It also provides visible exact-entry and step-button alternatives, which are not supplied by that source component. These local requirements must not be removed when taking visual inspiration from it.

Royal Navy's [Storybook accessibility configuration](https://github.com/Royal-Navy/design-system/blob/13163badc75f09695bb48968a0f82928eead6d35/packages/react-component-library/src/a11y/storyAccessibilityConfig.ts) disables its `color-contrast` rule for Range Slider. This is not proof of a particular contrast failure, but it prevents treating the presence of its stories as evidence that this check passed. Test the actual local colours and interactions independently.

The package release was published on 11 November 2026. The rolling Storybook index does not declare its deployed package version. The separate [Design guidance page](https://design-system.navy.digital.mod.uk/guidance/design) is explicitly legacy and describes Axure library versions, including 3.2; it must not be mistaken for the current React package version. This project imports neither the Royal Navy React dependency nor its branding, fonts or accessibility claims.

## Choose the right control

| Task | Use | Do not infer |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Adjust brightness or volume approximately | A native range plus a visible value and alternative input | That 0% means off or mute for every integration |
| Set an exact temperature or offset | Precise text entry and step buttons; a range may supplement them | A universal range, step, temperature unit or safe setting |
| Choose a named mode | Radios or a select with actual supported options | A numeric sequence between unrelated modes |
| Show a measurement | Text, a gauge or a chart with its unit and date | An editable slider merely because the value is numeric |
| Move a cover, valve or other mechanism | The [access and movement contract](../home-assistant/access-and-movement.html) and the actual supported position control | That moving a draft thumb is harmless or immediately confirms movement |
| Set two related bounds | Two separately labelled exact fields with ordering validation | That two overlapping slider thumbs are an accessible default |

Use the [continuous-controls pattern](../home-assistant/continuous-controls.html) for live updates, permissions, physical actions and reconciliation. This page owns the reusable input anatomy and reference appearance.

## Component anatomy and appearance

Keep a persistent device heading, a separately labelled reported value, a draft label, visible minimum/maximum/step instructions, the range, precise entry, step buttons and an explicit action. State the unit in the labels; do not rely on a coloured track or a suffix alone. Use the shared civica typography, ink input boundaries, quiet surrounding dividers and yellow-and-ink focus treatment.

The thumb remains round so it reads as something that moves along a track. Provide a control area at least 44 CSS pixels high, enough space for the thumb and focus ring, and wrapping step buttons. A square-card rule must not flatten the thumb into a badge or obscure its selected position. Do not use red/green position colouring to imply danger or success across an ordinary range. Respect forced colours instead of suppressing the user's palette.

The reference markup uses native `input type="range"`. Let the browser supply its slider role and numeric bounds. Do not add a second `role="slider"` wrapper or duplicate `aria-valuenow` that can drift. A meaningful `aria-valuetext` supplies the unit. Exact entry uses `type="text"`; [GOV.UK text-input guidance](https://design-system.service.gov.uk/components/text-input/) cautions against number inputs without evidence of need. The signed example omits `inputmode="decimal"` because some mobile keypads omit the minus sign.

## Complete examples

These examples use invented capabilities and readings. Changing a control updates a local draft. Submitting gives local feedback; it never sends a Home Assistant action or changes the reported observation. Without JavaScript the form controls are disabled and the explanatory content remains available.

### Brightness with three equivalent input methods

The precise field and step buttons provide alternatives to dragging. A minimum of 1% is an invented capability, not a rule for all lights. On/off remains a separate device action.

